{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c503","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07039-8c36-7981-972e-5e4ed62aa495"},"sequence":220,"recorded_at":1780531400000435,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"58f5c997-17a5-45b9-a711-d639aa7cbef7","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"58f5c997-17a5-45b9-a711-d639aa7cbef7","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"58f5c997-17a5-45b9-a711-d639aa7cbef7"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"e56292de-e8f1-4a85-8dd9-c6567e269ae3"},"task_id":"e56292de-e8f1-4a85-8dd9-c6567e269ae3","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"e56292de-e8f1-4a85-8dd9-c6567e269ae3","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-32jeti/TASK-260830-32jeti_review-verdict-round6.md`.\n1|# TASK-260830-32jeti — round-6 review verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED\n2|\n3|Reviewer run `RUN-260905-4b1178`. Candidate `CR-TASK-260830-32jeti-3` revision 3,\n4|base `57afcc6d`, candidate tree `aa190969ba39fee5234fbb10bbff2af68441c5a0`.\n5|Tree OID re-derived at the end of the review and matched exactly — every probe\n6|below was planted and reverted; the candidate is unmodified.\n7|\n8|**Verdict: changes requested (`to-dev`). Two blocking findings.**\n9|\n10|Round 5's single blocking finding (F1r) **is closed** and closed well. What\n11|blocks now is (F1) the residue the census cannot see, and (F2) a\n12|nondeterministic production defect on `Host.Call`'s primary path that this\n13|leaf's own harness does not detect.\n14|\n15|---\n16|\n17|## G-A — the census, attacked\n18|\n19|### The census is genuinely load-bearing for the shapes it scans\n20|\n21|Seven narrowing mutants planted **inside** the census itself; five killed.\n22|\n23|| # | Narrowing | Result |\n24|| --- | --- | ---: |\n25|| C3 | scan skips `spawn.go` | KILLED (`registered derivation \"spawnMembers\" names no production vocabulary`) |\n26|| C4 | drop the `ranged[name]` membership disjunct | KILLED |\n27|| C5 | drop the `indexed[name]` disjunct | KILLED |\n28|| C6 | drop the `unknownMember` disjunct | KILLED |\n29|| C7 | `valueShape` stops recognising `map[string]string` | KILLED |\n30|| C9 | plant an unregistered vocabulary in **build-tagged** `runner_windows.go` | KILLED |\n31|| C10 | control: same plant in `runner_unix.go` | KILLED |\n32|\n33|Both census directions are real, not decorative: C4–C7 redden through the\n34|*orphaned-registration* loop, which is the direction that would otherwise pass\n35|vacuously on a truncated scan.\n36|\n37|**Build-tag question answered affirmatively, by construction rather than by\n38|inheritance.** C9 plants `var bogusCensusVocab = map[string]bool{...}` plus a\n39|package-level index expression into `runner_windows.go`, a file `//go:build\n40|windows` excludes from the darwin build entirely. The census still finds it and\n41|fails. `os.ReadDir` + `parser.ParseFile(…, 0)` ignore build constraints, so the\n42|scan covers files the compiler never sees. C10 is the same plant in a compiled\n43|file and behaves identically.\n44|\n45|**Can `deriveMembershipVocabularies` return empty while `registered` is\n46|non-empty?** Confirmed by forcing it — C3 is exactly that shape (one file's\n47|vocabularies removed from `found`, registrations left standing) and the second\n48|loop catches it. The zero-registration floor and the duplicate-registration\n49|tripwire are correct but are not what carries this; the orphan loop is.\n50|\n51|### The blind spot is not empty — ratio 19 of 22\n52|\n53|Denominator derived **independently of the census**, by a separate AST walk of\n54|every package-level `var`/`const`/`type` in the 15 non-test files\n55|(`.temp/rev3-review/enum_vars.go`), plus a sweep for every `switch`, every\n56|function-local `[]string`/`map[string]bool` literal, every `slices.Contains` /\n57|`sort.Search` call, and every `init()`:\n58|\n59|- 27 package-level declarations of the three scanned shapes exist.\n60|- 8 are excluded by design, and **both exclusions verified independently**: each\n61|  of the seven `*Required` lists has exactly one production use and it is a\n62|  `missingMember` argument; `profileProviders` has zero production uses at all.\n63|- 19 remain — the census finds 19 and registers 19. **19/19 for its own shapes.**\n64|- **3 more closed vocabularies exist in production that the census cannot see**,\n65|  all `switch`-shaped, all with real production call sites, all unguarded.\n66|- (A fourth, `required := []string{…}` at `protocol.go:306`, is function-local\n67|  and equally invisible, but belongs to the `*Required` order-list class that is\n68|  excluded by design. Not counted.)\n69|\n70|No chained-equality vocabularies, no `slices.Contains`, no `sort.Search`, no\n71|`init()`-built sets, no `map[string]struct{}` — those predicted shapes are\n72|genuinely absent. The three that exist are `switch`/`default`.\n73|\n74|### F1 (BLOCKING) — three switch-shaped closed vocabularies admit an extra member with the suite green\n75|\n76|Each row: mutant widens the accepted set by one value; **whole `internal/provhost`\n77|package**, no `-run` mask.\n78|\n79|| Mutant | Vocabulary | Production call site | Suite |\n80|| --- | --- | --- | ---: |\n81|| C1 | envelope member set (6, §7.2) `protocol.go:328` | `checkResponseMembers` ← `DecodeResponse` `protocol.go:416` | **SURVIVED 12/12** |\n82|| C2 | execution profile (2, §7.7) `profile.go:57` | `ProfileMapping` ← `checkSpawnProfileMapping` ← `DecodeSpawnPlan` `spawn.go:100` | **SURVIVED 12/12** |\n83|| C8 | transaction state (4, §7.5) `status.go:25` | `validStatusState` ← `DecodeStatusOutcome` `status.go:126` | **SURVIVED 22/24** |\n84|\n85|C8's two kills in 24 were **not** the status gate — both were\n86|`TestExecRunnerReportsCrashExit`, i.e. finding F2 below. Per the map-iteration\n87|rule I ran every survivor repeatedly rather than once; C8 is a clean survivor\n88|and its occasional \"kill\" is the flake, which is exactly the coin-flip the rule\n89|exists to catch.\n90|\n91|**These are real acceptances, not no-ops.** A probe driving each production\n92|entry point refuses all three on the shipped tree and accepts all three under\n93|the mutant:\n94|\n95|```\n96|shipped:  REFUSED provider_protocol_error … unknown member\n97|          REFUSED invalid_config … profile mapping names an unknown profile\n98|          REFUSED integrity_failure … status state is not a registry member\n99|mutant:   ACCEPTED: ProfileMapping(codex, bogus) = \"--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox\"\n100|          ACCEPTED: response envelope carrying \"bogus\" decoded without refusal\n101|          ACCEPTED: DecodeStatusOutcome state = \"bogus\"\n102|```\n103|\n104|C2 is the sharpest: an unknown profile resolves to the **unrestricted flag**\n105|rather than refusing, and `profile.go:5-10` states the opposite rule in the\n106|file's own words — *\"unknown providers and unknown profiles are refused, never\n107|defaulted.\"* At `spawn.go:100` that widening makes `DecodeSpawnPlan` validate a\n108|launch/resume plan's `profile_mapping` against the yolo flag for a profile the\n109|contract never defined.\n110|\n111|Why the existing tests miss all three: each gate **is** witnessed, but only for\n112|one specific rejected value — `\"diagnostics\"` for the envelope\n113|(`refusal_arm_inventory_test.go:461`), `{\"\", \"YOLO\", \"yolo \", \"unrestricted\",\n114|\"standard-yolo\"}` for the profile (`profile_test.go`). Witnessing a rejected\n115|value proves the arm is reachable; it does not pin the **accepted** set. That is\n116|precisely the round-2 finding class, one level up.\n117|\n118|**The claim in the tree is stronger than what ships.**\n119|`closed_vocabulary_census_test.go:18` opens *\"This file closes the\n120|closed-vocabulary class by construction.\"* It closes the class for\n121|package-level `[]string` / `map[string]bool` / `map[string]string`. The comment\n122|enumerates its out-of-scope items carefully (`*Required`, `profileProviders`,\n123|`[]Operation`) but never names `switch`-shaped vocabularies, so the residue\n124|reads as empty when it is three. Either shape counts as a fix: extend the\n125|derivation to `switch` bodies with a refusing `default`, or register these three\n126|explicitly — but the \"by construction\" sentence must not stand over an unstated\n127|blind spot that has live instances in it.\n128|\n129|---\n130|\n131|## F2 (BLOCKING) — `ExecRunner` discards a judged result on a stdin `EPIPE`; valid response frames are lost\n132|\n133|Found while stabilising C8. Not introduced by round 3 — this is leaf-2 code at\n134|`8fbc052` — but it is this leaf's finding: the conformance harness exists to\n135|exercise `Host.Call`, the defect is on `Host.Call`'s primary path, and the test\n136|that touches it is the one that flakes.\n137|\n138|`runner.go:132-134` and `141-143`:\n139|\n140|```go\n141|if writeErr != nil {\n142|        return Result{}, writeErr\n143|}\n144|```\n145|\n146|This fires **before stdout is ever considered**, whenever the stdin write races\n147|the plugin's exit and takes `EPIPE`. `Host.Call` then reports\n148|`provider_process_failed: runner reported no result`.\n149|\n150|Two doc comments in the same file state the opposite rule:\n151|\n152|- `runner.go:123-126` — *\"A stdin write failure after the process ran is still a\n153|  result when the process produced one… Report write and close failures only\n154|  when no result can be judged; otherwise the frame governs.\"*\n155|- `runner.go:208-209` — *\"a valid frame governs regardless of lateness or exit\n156|  status.\"*\n157|\n158|Measured at the production entry point (`Host{Runner: ExecRunner{}}.Call`),\n159|two independent runs:\n160|\n161|| Plugin shape | Correct outcome | Wrong outcome | Rate |\n162|| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n163|| answers a **valid frame**, exits without reading stdin | 390 / 298 accepted | **10 / 2 frames discarded** | 0.7 – 2.5% |\n164|| `cat >/dev/null; exit 3` (the shipped test's own script) | 368 / 294 `plugin exited without a response` | **32 / 6 `runner reported no result`** | 2 – 8% |\n165|\n166|The first row is the severe one: a plugin returns a **well-formed, correct,\n167|correlated §7.2 response** and the host throws it away and fails the call. The\n168|second is a diagnostic misclassification of a crash.\n169|\n170|Consequences:\n171|\n172|- `go test ./internal/provhost/ -count=1` on the **clean candidate tree** failed\n173|  **3 of 30** runs under load (`TestExecRunnerReportsCrashExit/crash_exit`:\n174|  `error = provider_process_failed: … runner reported no result, want detail\n175|  containing \"plugin exited without a response\"`). A further 20 runs were green\n176|  and `go test ./...` was green 8/8 — the flake is load-sensitive, so a green\n177|  suite is a scheduling outcome, not a property. 3 failures in 50 isolated runs.\n178|- Every single-run KILL verdict in this task's rounds 4–6 evidence carries that\n179|  same false-kill probability. It did not change any conclusion here — I re-ran\n180|  every survivor — but it means single-run mutant results in the chain are\n181|  weaker than they read.\n182|- `LOGBOOK.md` 0926 records *\"`go test ./...` 15/15 green\"* as a property. It is\n183|  a single observation of a nondeterministic suite. Fix F2 and the claim\n184|  becomes true; until then it should not be stated flatly.\n185|\n186|The fix is what the comment already specifies: when `command.ProcessState` is\n187|available, judge the result — a non-empty frame governs, and an empty stdout\n188|with a known exit code is `plugin exited without a response` — and surface\n189|`writeErr` only when nothing can be judged. Whatever the fix, it needs a\n190|negative test that fails when a valid frame is discarded; a plugin that answers\n191|without reading stdin is a shape `ExecRunner.Run`'s own comment at\n192|`runner.go:75-77` already anticipates, so it is in scope by the package's\n193|own reckoning.\n194|\n195|---\n196|\n197|## G-B — full sweep, no resurrection\n198|\n199|All nine round-4 batches replayed against rev3, whole-package runs,\n200|`cp`-aside restore with sha256 verification after every row.\n201|\n202|| Battery | Rows | Killed | Survived |\n203|| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n204|| b1 | 15 | 15 | 0 |\n205|| b1b | 20 | 19 | 1 (`Q4b-backgroundNull`) |\n206|| b2 | 21 | 21 | 0 |\n207|| b2r | 11 | 11 | 0 |\n208|| b3 | 20 | 20 | 0 |\n209|| b4 | 25 | 24 | 0 (`K6` NOT_APPLIED, stale path) |\n210|| b5 | 7 | 7 | 0 |\n211|| b6 | 2 | 2 | 0 |\n212|| b7 | 1 | 0 | 1 (`C9b-newArmViaWrapperOnly`) |\n213|| `k6fix` (corrected `K6` path) | 1 | 1 | 0 |\n214|| **total** | **122** | **120** | **2** |\n215|\n216|**120 of 122. The two survivors are `Q4b` and `C9b` — the two stated bounds,\n217|name for name. Zero resurrections, zero new survivors.** Identical to round 5.\n218|\n219|Round-5 pack replayed too:\n220|\n221|- `probe_widen.json` — **12 of 14**. W1–W10 all KILLED, and each of W1–W9 is red\n222|  **three independent ways**: its derivation test, its census subtest, and its\n223|  `TestClosedMemberSetsRefuseExtraMembers` production-entry-point row. That is a\n224|  real closure of F1r, not a one-witness patch. R2/R4 killed. R1/R3 survive —\n225|  the two stated behaviour-preserving bounds.\n226|- `probe_failclosed.json` — **4 of 4** killed (V1 blank window, V2 no code span,\n227|  V3 bad marker, V4 joined-member collision; V4 now dies to the derivation alone\n228|  thanks to the `reflect.DeepEqual` fix).\n229|\n230|**Leaf 1 (`TASK-260830-2890sd`, `internal/provider`)** — subtree OID\n231|`308a2f9f2622fbd645298717d9eb03bd0d12483a`, byte-identical to the round-5\n232|measurement. Untouched; no resurrection is structurally possible.\n233|\n234|**Leaf 2 (`TASK-260830-qcosxq`, provhost protocol)** — I reconstructed the rev2\n235|tree from its own patch and got `ca45ddf611c18ca58d573e6e13f5238904f109a8`,\n236|exactly the OID the round-5 probe pack records, so the comparison is exact. The\n237|rev2→rev3 delta is **test-only**: `+688` new lines\n238|(`closed_vocabulary_census_test.go`), a 9-line edit, and a LOGBOOK entry. **Zero\n239|production bytes changed.** Leaf 2's 102/112 replay therefore stands unchanged,\n240|and the 122-battery replay above confirms it empirically at the provhost level.\n241|\n242|The 9-line edit is a **strengthening**, not a relaxation:\n243|`requireVocabulary` moves from `fmt.Sprintf(\"%v\", …)` string comparison to\n244|`reflect.DeepEqual`, which is what kills V4.\n245|\n246|---\n247|\n248|## G-C — bounds carried by the tree, not only the board\n249|\n250|| Bound | In the tree | Verified |\n251|| --- | --- | --- |\n252|| `*Required` last-entry drops behaviour-preserving | `closed_vocabulary_census_test.go:36-39` | R1/R3 survive; each `*Required` has exactly one production use, a `missingMember` argument — the drop leaves the body refusing |\n253|| `profileProviders` out of scope | same comment block | zero production uses, confirmed by grep |\n254|| new arm reachable only through a wrapper stays blind | `refusal_arm_inventory_test.go:128-133` | `C9b` survives, as recorded |\n255|| census floor is a duplicate tripwire, not load-bearing | census both-direction loops | confirmed — C4–C7 die to the orphan loop, not the floor |\n256|| `provhost` does not import `internal/provider` | — | `go list -deps`: catalog, scalar, canonicaljson, axerror only |\n257|| `provhost` has no importers | — | no `internal/provhost` import outside the package |\n258|| no top-level `cmd/` | — | absent |\n259|| stderr cap, error-detail contents, receipt ownership, §8 cell values, §8.2 roots | `doc.go` \"Stated bounds\" | present and accurate |\n260|\n261|The one bound **missing** from the tree is F1's: the census's blindness to\n262|`switch`-shaped vocabularies, currently covered by a \"closes the class by\n263|construction\" claim instead.\n264|\n265|---\n266|\n267|## Gates\n268|\n269|| Gate | Result |\n270|| --- | --- |\n271|| `go vet ./...` | exit 0 |\n272|| `gofmt -l internal/` | clean |\n273|| `go test ./... -count=1` | green 8/8 runs |\n274|| `go test ./internal/provhost/ -count=1` | **red 3 of 50 runs** (F2) |\n275|| `go test ./internal/provhost/ -race -count=1` | green |\n276|| coverage | provhost 86.5%, provider 97.0% |\n277|| candidate tree after review | `aa190969…` unchanged |\n278|\n279|---\n280|\n281|## What closes this round\n282|\n283|1. **F1** — bring the three `switch`-shaped vocabularies (`checkResponseMembers`\n284|   envelope, `ProfileMapping` profile, `validStatusState` state) under a\n285|   derivation, or register them explicitly; and correct the\n286|   \"closes the class by construction\" sentence so the residue is stated. C1, C2\n287|   and C8 must go red.\n288|2. **F2** — stop discarding a judgeable `Result` on a stdin `EPIPE`, so a valid\n289|   frame governs as both doc comments already promise; add a negative test that\n290|   fails when a valid frame is discarded, and restate the LOGBOOK green-suite\n291|   claim once the suite is deterministic.\n292|\n293|Everything else in this leaf is in good shape. The census is well-built, F1r is\n294|genuinely closed three ways over, and nothing regressed anywhere in the story.\n295|\n296|Replay pack attached as `TASK-260830-32jeti_review-round6-probe-pack.tgz`."}}}
